3. Field recordings in sounds/field/
------------------------------------

The animal calls in the Dawn chorus kit and in the Seashore and Camargue
night ambiences are real recordings, not synthesis. Synthesis is convincing
for surf, fire and wind -- they are filtered noise and nothing else -- and
unconvincing for animals: a gull is a resonant body with a vocal tract, and
a songbird is a syrinx, which is two independent sound sources in one throat.

All of them are public domain or CC0, so they carry no attribution
requirement and no share-alike obligation. They are credited anyway, because
somebody stood in a marsh at dawn to make them.

  gull1.ogg, gull2.ogg, gull3.ogg
      European Herring Gull (Larus argentatus), flight calls.
      Recorded at Carolles, Manche, France by Sonothèque ADVL.
      CC0 1.0 Universal (public domain dedication).
      https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:XC707075_-_European_Herring_Gull_-_Larus_argentatus.mp3

  tit1.ogg
      Great Tit (Parus major), song, recorded in a birch in Southern Finland
      by Oona Raisanen (Mysid). Public domain.
      https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Parus_major.ogg

  finch1.ogg
      Common Chaffinch (Fringilla coelebs), song, recorded in a spruce in
      Southern Finland by Oona Raisanen (Mysid). Public domain.
      https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Fringilla_coelebs_short.ogg

  chiff1.ogg, chiff2.ogg
      Common Chiffchaff (Phylloscopus collybita), song, Southern Finland,
      by Oona Raisanen (Mysid). Public domain.
      https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Phylloscopus_collybita.ogg

  wren1.ogg
      Eurasian Wren (Troglodytes troglodytes), song, by Sogning Norway.
      Public domain.
      https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Troglodytes_troglodytes.ogg

  frog1.ogg, frog2.ogg
      Edible frogs (Pelophylax kl. esculentus), chorus.
      Recorded in May at Krzemienica, Łódź Voivodeship, Poland by WrS.tm.pl.
      Public domain.
      https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Edible_frogs,_sounds._May._The_Village_of_Krzemienica,_%C5%81%C3%B3d%C5%BA_Voivodeship,_Poland.ogg

  heron1.ogg, heron2.ogg
      Great Blue Heron (Ardea herodias), call.
      Author unknown; published as public domain.
      https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Great_Blue_Heron.ogg

Each file here is an excerpt of the original: cut to the call, made mono,
high-passed to remove wind rumble, faded at both ends so it cannot click,
level-matched, and encoded as Vorbis at 22.05 kHz. Twelve clips, 120 kB in
total. The originals are linked above and are unmodified at those addresses.

A note on what is NOT here. Recordings of crickets, owls, surf and fire were
available only under CC BY-SA, whose share-alike term would attach to any
adaptation of them. Those are synthesised instead. For surf and fire that is
no loss -- they are filtered noise and nothing else, which is the half
synthesis does well. For the cricket and the owl it is a compromise, and it is
named here rather than hidden.

A warning for anyone repeating this search. Commons is full of
word-pronunciation recordings whose titles read like species names: a search
for "Gryllidae" returns Jer-Cricket.ogg, which is somebody saying the English
word "cricket" in Jerriais, and a search for blackbirds returns De-Amsel.ogg,
which is the German word for one. Both are public domain, both would have
shipped on a check of the filename, and neither is an animal. Take the licence
from the file's own metadata and the content from its description.

5. Generated kit plates -- demo/plates/
---------------------------------------

This directory is empty in the repository and may stay that way. The kit cards
are drawn by src/visual/engrave.js, which needs nothing and depends on nothing.

tools/make-plates.mjs can instead produce them with an image model, and anyone
who runs it should know what that means. The images are made by a third-party
service from prompts held in that file; their terms, not this project's,
govern what may be done with the results. Nothing generated is redistributed
here, and the tool is inert without an API key.

What is stored, if they are generated, is not the returned image: it is a mask
carrying the line work in its alpha channel, so that the plate takes its colour
from the palette at draw time. The originals are left in art/plates-raw/, which
is not published.

